40 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427227 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427229 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427233 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1077 "selection" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427229 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427240 1079 #uuid "efbed3e7-aa8b-5df8-a8c8-3e66cfa0aab7" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427239 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1069 "q-149674" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{6X}{X-2}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x-2)}{36},\\,3\\le x\\le 6.\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1072 #uuid "6fb74db2-0900-5354-80cf-fff8a70001a9" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427241 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{6x}{x-2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{6X}{X-2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{6x}{x-2}\\cdot \\frac{x(x-2)}{36}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{3}^{6}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[216-27] \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427237 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1069 "q-137057" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{1-x}}{e(e-1)},\\,-1\\le x\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1072 #uuid "9b261dd8-6dbc-5eeb-9c4c-65cef3fee9b6" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427242 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{X}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{1-x}}{e(e-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{e}{e(e-1)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{\\cancel{e}}{\\cancel{e}(e-1)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{x}{e-1}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427235 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427236 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427238 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1069 "q-275747" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1071 "A stick of length $4$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y=Y(X)$ denote the length of the longest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$\\text{E}[Y]={\\int }_{0}^{2}$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{d}x\\,\\,+\\,\\,{\\int }_{2}^{4}$\n{{field-2}} $\\text{d}x$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is  \n{{field-3}}." 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1072 #uuid "c6838816-09f0-5c1d-bec0-f98c72a4afc6" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427243 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x)$, the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(x)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\text{ }\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability density function of $X.$ \n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is chosen uniformly from $[0,4]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\le x<4.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $4-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y$, is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=\\text{max}(X,4-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\text{max}(x,4-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le 2$, then $x\\le 4-x$, so the longer piece has length $4-x$,\n\n- if $2<x\\le 4$, then $x>4-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4-x, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ x, & 2<x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[\\text{max}(X,4-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(8-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $3.$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427234 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1069 "q-136886" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1071 "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below.  Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{\\sqrt{X}}{2}].$\n\n| $x$ | $100$ | $144$ | $196$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{21}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ |" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1072 #uuid "ca61cd95-d418-51ad-82e4-b4ca600ba389" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427244 1074 "For a discrete random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}r(x)f(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{\\sqrt{X}}{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}\\,f(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{100}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{21}+\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{196}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{7} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{21}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+7\\cdot \\frac{3}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{21}+2+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427240 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1069 "q-149665" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1071 "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below.  Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+3X].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ | $0.2$ |" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1072 #uuid "e0cd43d2-a8c5-5b4f-91a3-acfe37612344" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true] [17592186427245 1074 "For a discrete random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}r(x)f(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}+3x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+3X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,f(x) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1)\\cdot 0.1+({2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2)\\cdot 0.7+({3}^{2}+3\\cdot 3)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.7+18\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.4+7+3.6 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534597 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07ce31ef-1026-5bac-b533-845af528009d" 17592186427211 "answer-0f37406a-2823-57df-a27f-cc68d751f826" 17592186427212 "answer-144535bd-591c-55e5-916f-107b9f40b231" 17592186427213 "answer-147b8699-cedd-5620-a33a-bc1eb2278482" 17592186427214 "answer-1c7ffff2-bddf-5a16-acff-6a914e7b8c64" 17592186427215 "answer-223de790-dfba-55c4-8bd1-66a556e2e641" 17592186427216 "answer-4563220b-0270-5956-bc6e-991ea7a91c5c" 17592186427217 "answer-4a1ee5b3-ff9a-54ce-90f4-e6472056f769" 17592186427218 "answer-4b7dfd6b-8244-5f0b-89eb-548bcdf54c36" 17592186427219 "answer-5c84ebae-5440-5360-8ccf-0ae76d0005c7" 17592186427220 "answer-6e5c7dfb-c4c4-5228-ad6a-6b954f0ed129" 17592186427221 "answer-725ceca2-02f0-5e62-bfa6-bc19153d5a6a" 17592186427222 "answer-821b56ee-1791-545f-9a5b-4a7661af16bf" 17592186427223 "answer-8e149b97-9800-5d1c-8e2c-2de5804a8dc6" 17592186427224 "answer-9e89bb30-4275-59bb-a5d8-800091c53baf" 17592186427225 "answer-ad4675a7-924f-5b58-a5fe-73a6d7057946" 17592186427226 "answer-b1844074-d8ff-52d6-ae1a-39ca1706674d" 17592186427227 "answer-ba5ed202-2af3-5ccc-ae53-c7b8c1475c54" 17592186427228 "answer-bee5da54-4a99-5944-8aac-1eecb79faf68" 17592186427229 "answer-c0fe770a-bcd4-56bf-9000-093dcffd3bfd" 17592186427230 "answer-e04c1314-faf4-5e09-96c9-541a0737c37b" 17592186427231 "answer-f329f9a9-a752-5140-9d10-c2ac0622bcee" 17592186427232 "answer-f7ae10f3-6a36-55af-803b-06fea5e0400d" 17592186427233 "answer-field-03fce717-63e3-5403-a87a-5e07f58b344b" 17592